<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mizpah in Benjamin</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mizpah in Benjamin was an ancient town north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, usually identified with Tell en-Nasbeh. The Bible describes it as a gathering place of the tribes and a border fortress between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a> and Israel. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonians">Babylonians</a> destroyed Jerusalem in 587 or 586 BCE it became the seat of the governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gedaliah">Gedaliah</a>, and excavations show that it continued to flourish while Jerusalem lay in ruins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mizpah-in-benjamin.jpg" alt="Mizpah in Benjamin" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: דניאל צבי · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tel_a-Nasba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Babylonians appointed Gedaliah governor at <b>Mizpah</b>, but he was assassinated.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-judah">Kingdom of Judah · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
